Shared per-channel cache of raw MPEG-TS and what it makes possible: recording
from the cache (a recording started late begins at the programme's start), HTSP
timeshift through that same cache, recording a programme still held in it,
RAM-then-disk storage, keeping an idle channel tuned so its cache survives a
channel change, a DVR-driven cache warmup, and our fixes on top.

tvheadend/tvheadend#2249, the whole of it: the 12 commits of th0ma7/tvheadend
branch record-from-buffer (1ee51e1f3, on master a4a1ce5ee), rebased onto the
package base as 15068a978..cb90a7e43.

The branch now also carries the web interface's view of the shared caches,
which used to be patch 401: the caches in use on Home, a Status > Caches tab,
and the cache column and panel of Status > Subscriptions, with the status API
behind them.  Its history is regrouped as well: LiBe-net's seven commits as he
wrote them, the fixes on top of them as seven commits by area, and the
interface as one commit above those.  SonarCloud's findings on that state are
answered in the commits they belong to, and a recording's span on the cache
bar now starts where the recorder starts writing -- the padded start, not the
tuner's warm-up 30 s before it.  The caches share the size budget evenly, and
the RAM under "RAM only", the room moving gradually as a newcomer fills it;
the budget meters show it: under "RAM only" or with no RAM, the meter that
holds the caches is split by cache, a colour each -- the same in every
view -- and the bars are scaled by the budget.  With RAM and a disk, a cache
writes everything to disk and keeps its newest part in RAM as well.  The
caches follow the timeshift settings as they change, and turning the shared
cache off lets go of the channels kept tuned for it.

Patch 300 is gone: #2206, the stable stream/PID mapping 400 used to sit on, is
upstream as of this base.  `git rebase` dropped its three commits on its own,
"patch contents already upstream", so 400 now carries the branch entire and
there is no longer a 300 to drop it with.

Beyond what 400 held before, this snapshot carries the answers to a user
report and to two review passes:

  - a client is no longer offered the part of the cache that predates its own
    subscription *by default*.  It was reported as the oldest seekable point
    and then served as negative timestamps, which a player cannot order -- a
    frozen picture with sound, until playback reached the moment the viewer
    tuned in.  Putting the client's clock at the cache instead fixed that and
    shifted Kodi's guide by the cache depth, because pvr.hts anchors stream
    position zero at the first packet it receives; that attempt is reverted
    here and the range is clamped instead.  LiBe-net then reported Kodi
    ordering negative positions correctly, so the clamp is now a choice:
    "Cache seek before tune-in" (expert, off) lifts it for clients known to
    handle them.  Recording from the cache is unaffected either way.
  - a retrospective recording can no longer be started a second time while it
    is already running, which is a candidate for the duplicate recording seen
    on the bench; a cache-owned recording now ends on the gate's stop whatever
    the last error was, instead of leaving its thread waiting and its tuner
    held; the cache holds a reference to the service it caches; the replay
    gate's state is no longer read while another thread may be writing it.
  - the play position is written only against a recording that has a file to
    measure from, and only on one this connection may write.
  - a null title no longer reaches the recorder's start trace, which older GCC
    rejects under -Werror=format-overflow=.
  - the whole cache is offered, not just the configured timeshift period.
    svcbuf_expire() keeps blocks a reader still needs however old they are, so
    a cache feeding a recording reaches back to the programme's start whatever
    the period says; svcts_oldest() applied the period a second time and hid
    exactly that data, while the in-progress recording played it back without
    complaint.  Found on a film recorded from 18:29 and tuned live at 20:54.
  - a client is told its seek range exists even when it starts at zero.
    svcts_oldest() answers 0 both for "no cache" and for a range clamped to the
    client's own zero, and svcts_fill_status() read the second as the first, so
    the ordinary shared-cache case -- a cache older than the subscription --
    advertised no seekable range at all while svcts_skip() would have accepted
    the seek.  Reported by CodeRabbit.
  - the epg genre-list fix is gone from this patch: #2160 is merged, so that
    commit left the branch on its own at the next rebase, and 200 is dropped
    with it.
  - two of LiBe-net's own, cherry-picked from his pr2249-warmup-fixes branch:
    a scheduled warmup stays reschedulable after a higher-priority
    subscription takes its tuner, and scheduled warmups sit above EPG, scan
    and mapper activity while staying below live TV and DVR.  His third commit
    on that branch, which drops the clamp outright, is deliberately not taken:
    the setting above is what replaces it.

